1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,022
Reelearn. A minute a day.

2
00:00:02,072 --> 00:00:03,449
Remembered for months.

3
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:10,847
Frontline teams in Saudi retail and warehouses never open the training portal.

4
00:00:10,897 --> 00:00:14,781
Courses are long, in the wrong language, and forgotten within a week.

5
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:17,631
But everyone already watches short vertical video.

6
00:00:17,681 --> 00:00:21,622
So we made learning look like that, and made it stop after one minute.

7
00:00:23,450 --> 00:00:25,082
This is the company console.

8
00:00:25,132 --> 00:00:28,447
Noura, the learning lead, signs in with a one-time code.

9
00:00:28,497 --> 00:00:29,769
No passwords anywhere.

10
00:00:29,819 --> 00:00:32,653
Her dashboard shows how the company is learning:

11
00:00:32,703 --> 00:00:36,738
who is active, how much is remembered, and what needs her attention.

12
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:45,058
In the AI Reel Studio she describes a topic, or uploads a document, a talk, or a podcast.

13
00:00:45,108 --> 00:00:51,669
She picks the topic, the languages and the style, and the price in credits appears before anything runs.

14
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:03,267
Then the pipeline goes to work on Meta Model API.

15
00:01:03,317 --> 00:01:06,426
Muse Spark writes each script and its recall question.

16
00:01:06,476 --> 00:01:08,649
Muse Image paints a vertical keyframe.

17
00:01:08,699 --> 00:01:12,159
Muse Voice Transcribe turns talks and podcasts into moments.

18
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,783
Moments later the storyboard is ready:

19
00:01:15,833 --> 00:01:19,985
three reels, each with a script, a question, and an Arabic variant.

20
00:01:20,035 --> 00:01:24,689
She opens one, rewrites a line, and the phone preview updates as she saves.

21
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:35,473
Nothing reaches employees on the word of an AI alone.

22
00:01:35,523 --> 00:01:38,140
Publishing is locked until two people approve:

23
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:40,865
learning and development, and a subject expert.

24
00:01:40,915 --> 00:01:43,300
The database itself refuses anything less.

25
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:45,735
Then it goes live to the stores she picks.

26
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:53,152
Now Sara, on the shop floor.

27
00:01:53,202 --> 00:01:56,595
She signs in with her phone number and a WhatsApp code.

28
00:01:56,645 --> 00:01:58,222
Today's minute is waiting:

29
00:01:58,272 --> 00:02:00,037
two new reels and one review.

30
00:02:00,087 --> 00:02:03,041
She swipes up, like every app she already loves.

31
00:02:03,091 --> 00:02:04,919
After each reel, one question.

32
00:02:04,969 --> 00:02:06,922
Get it right, and it turns mint.

33
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:13,180
Get it wrong, and there is no red, just the right answer, and the question comes back in a few days.

34
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:15,246
She cannot swipe past a question.

35
00:02:15,296 --> 00:02:17,749
And after the last card, it simply ends.

36
00:02:17,799 --> 00:02:18,688
Done for today.

37
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:35,679
Her manager sees who is ready on each topic, never individual answers.

38
00:02:35,729 --> 00:02:39,592
And HR exports the evidence the national training fund asks for.

39
00:02:44,450 --> 00:02:51,020
Under the hood: a Rust API, Postgres with row level security for every tenant, Redis caching, a Next.

40
00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:56,002
js console and a Flutter app, covered by nearly two hundred automated tests.

41
00:02:56,052 --> 00:02:57,641
Reelearn. A minute a day.

42
00:02:57,691 --> 00:02:59,083
Remembered for months.
